        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Neoplasia, Carcinogenesis, and Tumor Biology",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "Two facts govern the guide. First, **malignancy is defined by capability, not by size or\nspeed** — the properties that make a neoplasm \"cancer\" are the ability to **invade** adjacent\ntissue and to **metastasize** to distant sites, not how big or fast it is. A slow, small\nmalignancy is still cancer; a large, fast benign growth is not. Second, **the capabilities are\n*acquired*, one at a time, by evolution** — the \"hallmarks\" framework (Section 4) is a list of\nthe barriers a clone must overcome, and carcinogenesis (Section 5) is the process of\novercoming them."
    }
